    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.pick\n\n### Description\n\nWith `mini.pick`, I can get rid of many key bindings and instead use a picker to choose and execute\n\nThat got me thinking: for each picker, there are often many actions. Instead of binding a key for every action, why not use another picker to choose from those actions? This way, I can eliminate most of the less frequently used key bindings and only need to remember one shortcut.\n\nso I think this action would be very nice to have by default.\n\nHere's what I've tried:\n\n```lua\nrequire(\"mini.pick\").setup({\n\tmappings = {\n\t\tpick_actions = {\n\t\t\tchar = \"<c-cr>\",\n\t\t\tfunc = function()\n\t\t\t\tlocal opts = require(\"mini.pick\").get_picker_opts()\n\t\t\t\tlocal actions = vim.tbl_keys(opts.mappings)\n\t\t\t\ttable.sort(actions)\n\t\t\t\trequire(\"mini.pick\").start({\n\t\t\t\t\tsource = {\n\t\t\t\t\t\titems = actions,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tchoose = function(item)\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-- <execute the chosen action in the previous picker>\n\t\t\t\t\t\tend,\n\t\t\t\t\t},\n\t\t\t\t})\n\t\t\tend,\n\t\t},\n\t},\n})\n```\n\nI haven't been able to implement `<execute the chosen action in the previous picker>`",
