        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nThis seems to be a bit of an overcomplication, mostly because it will duplicate already present functionality with built-in mappings.\n\n> This way, I can eliminate most of the less frequently used key bindings and only need to remember one shortcut.\n\nTo remind of present shortcuts (at least half of which, I'd argue, are aligned with similar actions outside of 'mini.pick') there is `<S-Tab>` to show info about current picker. To scroll down/up in that window, use `<C-f>` / `<C-b>`.\n\n> I haven't been able to implement `<execute the chosen action in the previous picker>`\n\nAnd probably for a good reason, because it would need to save previous picker (as there can only be one active picker at a time) and reimplement [all built-in actions](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/51cec14696190ad5b61e6c559ae558c703b71906/lua/mini/pick.lua#L2651-L2717) do execute in the context of that picker. I am afraid, making this easier (or even possible without extreme hacks) is out of scope of 'mini.pick'.\n\nClosing as not planned.",
